<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Soviet–Lithuanian Peace Treaty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1920">1920</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanian-soviet-war">Lithuanian–Soviet War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Soviet–Lithuanian Peace Treaty was signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on 12 July 1920, ending the war between Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. Russia recognised Lithuanian independence and Lithuanian claims to a large territory including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> handed over in August after taking it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>. Lithuania lost the city in October 1920, when Polish troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucjan-zeligowski">Lucjan Żeligowski</a> seized it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/soviet-lithuanian-peace-treaty.jpg" alt="Soviet–Lithuanian Peace Treaty" width="320" height="190" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Renata3 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lithuania_territory_1939-1940.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> declared independence on 16 February 1918, but the German occupation authority, Ober Ost, kept it from building a state until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> surrendered in November 1918. In late December Bolshevik forces pursuing the retreating Germans invaded, and a Soviet government under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vincas-mickevicius-kapsukas">Vincas Mickevičius-Kapsukas</a> was proclaimed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>, merged in February 1919 into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanian-byelorussian-soviet-socialist-republic">Litbel</a>. Polish and Lithuanian counterattacks ended it: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> seized Vilnius in April, and the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> were pushed from Lithuanian territory at the end of August 1919.</p><p>Georgy Chicherin proposed peace talks on 11 September 1919, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> held back, fearing harm to its relations with the Western powers, and talks opened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> only on 7 May 1920. The delegation under Tomas Naruševičius claimed the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">governorates</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kovno</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grodno">Grodno</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suwalki">Suwałki</a>, while Adolph Joffe's Soviet delegation offered recognition in return for a military alliance against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, which Lithuania refused. As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> recovered against Poland, Lithuania was pressed to sign, and its Constituent Assembly ratified the treaty on 8 August 1920.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>In 19 articles <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>'s independence without reservations and gave up all territorial claims. The border gave Lithuania the Vilnius Region with Breslauja, Gardinas, Lyda, Pastovys and Ašmena, but stopped at the village of Sztabin, leaving the Suvalkai Region undecided. Russia promised three million rubles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> and 107,000 hectares of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, freed Lithuania from debt and let refugees and prisoners of war go home; Lithuania held about 2,000 Russian prisoners and Russia about 150 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a>. A secret clause let Soviet forces move freely through the recognised territory while the war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> lasted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Lithuanian troops crossed the Foch line on 19 July 1920 to take the territories granted, clashing with Polish forces over Sejny, Augustów and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suwalki">Suwałki</a>. Poland accused <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> of letting Soviet troops pass through its territory, which Lithuania could not deny, and the historians Piotr Łossowski and Alfred E. Senn both question its claim to neutrality. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> brokered the Suwałki Agreement of 7 October 1920, two days before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucjan-zeligowski">Lucjan Żeligowski</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>.</p><p>The treaty was a breakthrough in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>'s search for international recognition, and interwar Lithuania held that its lawful borders were those the treaty drew. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> did not pay all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> or return the cultural property. Modern Belarusian historiography regards the cession of lands of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> as a unilateral Soviet act. The Lithuanian copy, taken abroad before the Soviet occupation of 1940, was transferred to the Lithuanian Central State Archive in 2021.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Soviet–Lithuanian Peace Treaty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Soviet-Lithuanian Peace Treaty</b> of July 1920 recognised Lithuanian claims to Vilnius, but in October 1920 Polish troops under General Lucjan Żeligowski seized the city in a staged mutiny.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania">Republic of Lithuania · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Soviet–Lithuanian Peace Treaty</b> of 12 July 1920 the Soviet Union recognised a fully independent Lithuania and its claims to the Vilnius Region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania-1918-1940">Republic of Lithuania (1918-1940) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
